Your job: Given the following original C source file, generate Frama-C WP-compatible ACSL annotations (function contract + loop invariant/loop assigns/loop variant) that allow Frama-C WP to prove the existing `//@ assert(sn == size);` assertion, WITHOUT changing any original C token or any original ACSL assertion.

Task: Prove the original benchmark assertions without changing program semantics.

Constraints from spec-bottomup skill:
- Keep every original C token unchanged. Keep the original ACSL `//@ assert(sn == size);` exactly as-is in its original location.
- Group loop invariant/assigns/variant clauses in ONE /*@ ... */ block before the while loop. Do not emit consecutive //@ loop clauses.
- Do not add admit/assume/axiom or use them to bypass anything. Not allowed.
- You may add an auxiliary loop invariant to help the proof, but must not weaken existing requirements.
- Include a meaningful function contract (requires/assigns/ensures) for the function.
- The loop: `while (i <= size) { i = i + 1; sn = sn + 1; }`. Note i starts at 1, sn starts at 0. The loop runs while i <= size, i.e. while i-1 <= size-1. Since i increments at end and sn increments, sn tracks (i-1). At loop exit i = size+1, so sn = size. The post-condition `if(sn != 0) { assert(sn == size); }` must be provable.
- Suggest loop invariant relating sn and i, e.g. `sn == i - 1` (careful with integer types). Provide loop invariant `1 <= i <= size+1`, `sn == i - 1`, loop assigns i, sn; loop variant size+1 - i;.
- For integer arithmetic, consider using `\forall integer` where relevant, but the key invariant is sn == i-1.
- Justify: The function contract requires size >= 1 (already given). Provides assigns i, sn; ensures \result... none (void function). 
- IMPORTANT: prove that after the while loop, sn == size. At exit, !(i <= size) means i >= size+1. Given loop invariant sn == i-1 and i <= size+1, at exit i == size+1, so sn == size. The invariant `sn == i - 1` combined with i <= size+1 and loop termination (i increasing) yields i == size+1 at exit. Ensure the invariant `1 <= i <= size+1` is established (initially i=1, ok) and preserved.

Here is the original file content (use EXACTLY these original tokens; only ADD ACSL annotations):

```c
/*@
requires size >= 1;
*/
void foo(int size) {
  int i = 1;
  int sn = 0;

  while (i <= size) {
    i = i + 1;
    sn = sn + 1;
  }
  // post-condition
  if(sn != 0) {
    //@ assert(sn == size);
  }
}
```

You may keep the original function requires clause and add loop annotations and an assigns/ensures clause to the function contract. Produce the fully annotated source. Return ONLY the JSON.

